# Jira create

## Gate

Ordinary coding stays **ticketless**. Do not create an issue, invent a key, or
open the tracker to “cover” work unless this turn explicitly authorizes it.

Create **only** when the user text clearly says to file one, for example:

- “make a Jira”, “file a Story/Bug/Task”, “open a ticket”, “create issues for…”

These do **not** authorize create — just implement on the current branch:

- “add X”, “fix Y”, “I think Z is broken”, naming a feature without filing

Naming an **existing** issue key means work that issue — it does not mean
create another one.

Ambiguity → **do not create**. Never ask whether to file unless the user
already raised filing.

## Create-only is the default

“Make a Jira / file a Story” authorizes **create only**. Put the rest of the
message into the description (see `atlassian-jira-description`) and **stop**. Do not
edit code, open a worktree, board the issue, or move status.

`global-execute-first` does **not** override create-only when the primary verb
this turn is file/ticket.

## Create-and-implement (explicit second verb only)

Same-turn implementation only when the user adds an explicit second verb, for
example: “file it and implement it”, “create the Story and build it”, “file it
then fix it”. Inference (“they described the work, so build it”) is forbidden.

## Defaults (never invent)

When create is authorized:

1. **Project** — host-configured default when present; else a project the
   operator or chat already named. Never invent a project key.
2. **Board / site / assignee** — same rule: configured or named only; skip
   board moves when unknown.
3. **Epic parent** — see below.

Prefer a connected Jira / Atlassian MCP when ready (`global-mcp-first`). Do not
invent credentials or side CLIs while MCP works.

## Epic parent

New Stories, Bugs, and Tasks should sit under an Epic unless epicless is
clearly warranted.

1. User-named epic wins.
2. Else host-configured default epic when set (verify it still exists and is
   an Epic).
3. Else search Epics in the resolved project and pick the best match from
   summary vs the work.
4. **Epicless** only when the user says so, when creating an Epic itself, when
   every candidate is clearly wrong, or when no reasonable epic exists and
   inventing one was not authorized.

Set parent via the tracker’s parent/epic fields after resolve. Do not hard-code
epic keys in policy.

## Types

| Type | Default create? | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Story | Yes | `atlassian-jira-story` |
| Bug | Yes | `atlassian-jira-bug` |
| Task | Yes | `atlassian-jira-task` |
| Epic | Only if user asks for an epic | `atlassian-jira-epic` |
| Sub-task | Only if user names sub-task | `atlassian-jira-subtask` |

## Create procedure

1. Resolve project / board / assignee / epic from host config or explicit
   context — never invent.
2. Apply the matching type rule + `atlassian-jira-description`.
3. Create via MCP (or host-approved CLI if no MCP).
4. Leave on **Backlog** / created state unless the user asked to board or
   start it. Status moves: `atlassian-jira-status`.
5. Return the issue key and browse link when the site URL is known.
